Server Side Request Forgery (SSRF)
1. What is SSRF
Server-Side Request Forgery occurs when a web application fetches a URL supplied by the user without validating where that request goes. The attacker tricks the server into sending requests to unintended destinations. 
Because the request originates from the server itself, the attacker can access resources that are normally inaccessible from the internet.
Typical SSRF targets include:
	• internal admin panels
	• internal APIs
	• databases exposed over HTTP
	• cloud metadata endpoints
	• localhost services
Example vulnerable feature:

POST /fetch-image
{
   "url": "https://example.com/image.jpg"
}
If the application fetches the URL directly, the attacker can replace it with:

http://localhost/admin
Now the server makes the request internally.


2. Why SSRF is Dangerous
SSRF turns the application server into a proxy controlled by the attacker.

Attacker
   |
   v
Web Application  -----> Internal Service
                     (not exposed to internet)
Because the request originates from inside the network, internal security boundaries are bypassed.
Attackers may be able to:
• access internal admin panels
• perform internal port scanning
• retrieve sensitive configuration data
• steal cloud credentials
In cloud environments, SSRF is especially dangerous because metadata services expose instance credentials. 
Example metadata endpoint:

http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/


3. How SSRF Works
Typical vulnerable flow:

User Input → Application → Server fetches URL → Response returned
Example:

GET /api/preview?url=http://example.com
Backend code:

response = requests.get(user_url)
return response.text
Attacker changes the URL:

http://127.0.0.1/admin
The server now accesses an internal resource.


4. Basic SSRF Attack Example
Vulnerable endpoint:

GET /fetch?url=
Attacker payload:

/fetch?url=http://localhost:8080/admin
Result:

Server → localhost:8080/admin
If the response is returned to the attacker, they can see internal data.


5. Cloud SSRF (Most Important Modern Attack)
Modern SSRF attacks usually target cloud metadata services.
Example AWS metadata service:

http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/
Attack payload:

http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/
If successful, attackers may obtain:
	• IAM credentials
	• instance tokens
	• cloud configuration data
These credentials can allow attackers to access cloud resources such as S3 or databases. 
This exact technique contributed to the Capital One breach, where SSRF exposed AWS credentials. 


6. SSRF Attack Types
6.1 Basic SSRF
Attacker directly accesses internal systems.
Example:

http://127.0.0.1:8080/admin


6.2 Blind SSRF
The attacker cannot see the response.
Instead they detect impact via:
	• DNS callbacks
	• timing differences
	• out-of-band channels
Example payload:

http://attacker.com/ssrf-test
If the server makes the request, the attacker sees it in logs.


6.3 File SSRF
Some libraries allow other protocols.
Example:

file:///etc/passwd
If allowed, the server may read local files.


6.4 Internal Network Scanning
Attackers use SSRF to scan internal networks.
Example:

http://10.0.0.5:8080
http://10.0.0.6:8080
http://10.0.0.7:8080
This reveals internal services.


7. Real World SSRF Entry Points
Most SSRF vulnerabilities appear in features that fetch external resources.
Common examples:
Image import

POST /upload
{
 "image_url":"http://example.com/image.png"
}


Webhook testing

POST /webhook/test
{
 "url":"http://example.com"
}


URL preview services

POST /preview
{
 "url":"http://example.com"
}


Document converters
Example:

PDF generator
HTML → PDF
The generator loads external resources.


8. Secure Architecture Defense
Strong defenses include:
1. Outbound network restrictions
The server should not be able to access internal addresses.
Block:

127.0.0.1
169.254.169.254
10.0.0.0/8
192.168.0.0/16


2. Allowlist URLs
Only allow trusted domains.
Example:

allowed_domains = ["images.example.com"]


3. Disable dangerous protocols
Reject:

file://
gopher://
ftp://


4. Use cloud metadata protection
AWS example:
Enable IMDSv2.
IMDSv2 requires a session token, making SSRF much harder. 


9. Secure Coding Pattern
Vulnerable Code

url = request.GET["url"]
response = requests.get(url)
return response.text


Secure Code

from urllib.parse import urlparse

url = request.GET["url"]
parsed = urlparse(url)

if parsed.hostname not in allowed_domains:
    raise Exception("Invalid URL")

response = requests.get(url)


10. Production Security Checklist
Before releasing features that fetch URLs:
Check:

□ URL allowlist enforced
□ Private IPs blocked
□ Metadata endpoint blocked
□ Redirects validated
□ Dangerous protocols disabled
□ DNS rebinding protection
□ Outbound firewall rules configured


